00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:04.820 align:start position:0%
since the advent of computers designing  complex machines and structures has  become way easier  computerized topology optimization can  simplify components of these modern  marvels even further reducing weight and  increasing efficiency but there's a  limit object sizes can't be very large  researchers at the Technical University  of Denmark have created a new software  tool that can visualize and optimize  complex shapes in high resolution just  as images on a computer screen are  broken up into pixels 3d images are made  up of boxes called voxels previous  computer design tools for this kind of  engineering could only generate designs  that were about 5 million voxels large  but with the giga voxel resolution of  the new system objects can be made up of  over 1 billion voxels so huge or highly  detailed objects can be optimized the  researchers say that their system scales  in both directions it can help optimize  massive engineering projects and smaller  nanoscale technologies the engineers put  the system through its paces by feeding  it a 27 meter long wing from a technical  model that resembles a Boeing triple7  airliner in a supercomputer crunched the  numbers for five days and produce a new  design a wing with a radical internal  structure that has kept solid through  curved wing spars and diagonal ribs the  new more hollow wing weighs 5% less than  the current Wings in use on the Boeing  triple7 such a reduction in weight means  the plane would consume up to 200 metric  tons less fuel per year but the wing  itself can be reasonably assembled  through any modern production process  however the researchers were able to  successfully 3d print a section of the  wing and new methods of additive  manufacturing could make fabricating  these designs easier in the future  you  [Music]  [Laughter]
 

